\section{Families}{

\code{gaussian()} and \code{student()} only - the two families with a real
residual, and exactly the two brms treats this way (\code{student()} gets
the multivariate-t analog, with one \code{nu} per group, so a predicted
\code{nu ~ ...} is refused). brms accepts the same spelling for other
families but fits a different model there: a latent Gaussian AR
process added to the linear predictor. That model has a spelling of
its own here - a random effect over the time factor, \code{+ ar1(factor(week) + 0 | subj)}, or \code{toep()} / \code{us()} for a freer lag
structure - and the refusal names it.
}

\section{Time points, gaps and ragged groups}{

The lag between two rows is the distance between their positions in
the GLOBAL set of time levels, so a group missing week 3 gets
\code{cor(week2, week4) = rho^2}. That is \code{nlme}'s reading
(\code{corAR1(form = ~ week | subj)}) and what the agreement tests pin
down; brms instead indexes \code{ar()}/\code{ma()}/\code{arma()}/\code{cosy()} by
position within the group and treats a missing row as no gap (only
its \code{unstr()} carries a time index). On complete balanced groups the
two coincide. Time levels that are whole numbers but not consecutive
warn, because the lag is then not the one the labels suggest.

Groups of different sizes are handled by construction: the density is
evaluated one PATTERN at a time (a pattern being a distinct set of
present time levels), so a balanced design costs one Cholesky per
gradient evaluation and ragged data costs one per distinct pattern.
Duplicate \verb{(gr, time)} pairs are refused, as they are in brms.
}

\section{What cannot be combined with it}{

The likelihood is a joint density over each group, so it no longer
factorizes into per-row contributions. \code{weights()}, \code{cens()},
\code{trunc()}, \code{se()} and \code{mi()} on the same response are therefore
refused, as are \code{rescor = TRUE}, mixture families,
\code{quadrature = TRUE}, \code{frm_simulate()} and
\code{residuals(type = "osa")}. brms refuses the same core set. Random
effects ARE allowed and are the point of the feature: the marginal
likelihood is a Laplace approximation over the modes with the
correlated residual density inside, which reproduces
\code{nlme::lme(random = ~ 1 | subj, correlation = corAR1())}.
}

\section{Where the parameters appear}{

On the internal (unconstrained) scale they are the \verb{thetaac_*} rows
of \code{\link[=confint.frmtmb_fit]{confint.frmtmb_fit()}} and of \code{vcov(full = TRUE)}. On the natural
scale, \code{summary()} prints them under "Within-group residual
correlation" and \code{\link[=confint_varcorr]{confint_varcorr()}} reports one row per parameter
under brms's names - \code{ar[1]}, \code{ma[1]}, \code{cosy},
\verb{cortime__<t1>__<t2>} - with a delta-method interval (Fisher-z for
the bounded ones, the identity for the coefficients of a
higher-order AR/MA process). \code{\link[=hypothesis]{hypothesis()}} sees them as \code{ar1},
\code{ma1}, \code{cosy} and \verb{cortime__<t1>__<t2>}. \code{\link[=autocor_matrix]{autocor_matrix()}} returns
the fitted \code{R}. They are NOT part of \code{\link[=VarCorr]{VarCorr()}}, which reports
random-effect blocks. \code{set_prior()} cannot target them yet.
}

\section{Divergence from brms}{

brms parameterizes \code{ar()}, \code{ma()} and \code{arma()} by the INNOVATION
standard deviation (its \code{cholesky_cor_ar1()} divides by
\code{1 - ar^2}), while \code{cosy()} and \code{unstr()} use the marginal one. Here
every structure uses the marginal \code{sigma}, so that \code{sigma()}, pearson
residuals and a \code{sigma ~ x} model mean one thing throughout. The
correlation parameters agree with brms exactly; the scales relate by
\code{sigma_marginal = sigma_innovation / sqrt(1 - phi^2)} for AR(1) and
\code{sigma_marginal = sigma_innovation * sqrt(1 + theta^2)} for MA(1).
brms also limits \code{cov = TRUE} to order one ("Covariance formulation
of ARMA structures is only possible for effects of maximal order
one"); higher \code{p} and \code{q} are supported here.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
d <- expand.grid(week = 1:5, subj = factor(1:30))
d$x <- rnorm(150)
e <- as.vector(vapply(1:30, function(i) {
  as.vector(stats::filter(rnorm(5), 0.6, "recursive"))
}, numeric(5)))
d$y <- 1 + 0.5 * d$x + e

fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x + ar(week, subj, cov = TRUE)) + gaussian(),
           data = d)
summary(fit)
autocor_matrix(fit)

# compound symmetry, and the unstructured correlation over the five
# weeks
frm(bf(y ~ x + cosy(week, subj)) + gaussian(), data = d)
frm(bf(y ~ x + unstr(week, subj)) + gaussian(), data = d)

# a random intercept alongside the correlated residual is allowed
frm(bf(y ~ x + (1 | subj) + ar(week, subj, cov = TRUE)) + gaussian(),
    data = d)
}
